WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.069
Physiological outcomes come from molecules interacting.

2
00:00:06.119 --> 00:00:11.937
Those interactions change function, and function is driven by changes in shape and structure.

3
00:00:11.987 --> 00:00:15.587
Pharmacology is what drugs do to that whole system.

4
00:00:15.637 --> 00:00:18.588
So how far can a chain of surrogates take us?

5
00:00:19.438 --> 00:00:25.776
Biology has a ladder of scales: sequence, structure, atoms in motion, cells and tissue.

6
00:00:25.826 --> 00:00:31.084
Machine learning now has a ladder alongside it, mostly open-weight, mostly new.

7
00:00:31.134 --> 00:00:37.014
Each rung swaps an expensive physical calculation for a learned one, and the catch is trust.

8
00:00:37.064 --> 00:00:40.119
In two days in Auckland, we climbed two rungs.

9
00:00:40.969 --> 00:00:44.617
Docking a billion molecules takes about a hundred days.

10
00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:49.588
So you train a surrogate on docking scores, and dock only what it ranks highest.

11
00:00:49.638 --> 00:00:58.047
We rebuilt that surrogate: VDSAI Lightning, an AttentiveFP graph network replacing AMPL's GraphConv stack.

12
00:00:58.097 --> 00:01:06.168
On three and a half million molecules it trained 13× faster, and after tuning it beat AMPL on every test metric.

13
00:01:06.218 --> 00:01:13.044
Then we scored 925 million molecules in under four and a half hours on a single workspace.

14
00:01:13.094 --> 00:01:21.748
Head to head on a 98-million-molecule pool, Lightning recovered 35% of the true docking top million.

15
00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:27.391
AMPL recovered 32%, ranking by size 2.6%, random picks 1.2%.

16
00:01:27.441 --> 00:01:31.737
And it used 381× fewer GPU hours.

17
00:01:31.787 --> 00:01:39.533
Two dockings of the same molecules correlate at only 0.3, so the surrogate is now chasing a noisy target.

18
00:01:40.383 --> 00:01:45.055
Rung two carries a molecular signal all the way up to physiology.

19
00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:52.392
Our pipeline turns a molecular dynamics trajectory into a model that a heart or kidney cell model can run.

20
00:01:52.442 --> 00:01:57.242
It measures diffusion, learns the free-energy landscape, finds the barriers,

21
00:01:57.242 --> 00:02:02.808
and builds a bond graph, a thermodynamic circuit, written out as standard CellML.

22
00:02:02.858 --> 00:02:09.022
The physics is in the architecture: the second law holds for any weights, at any point in training.

23
00:02:09.072 --> 00:02:16.131
An unconstrained network fits just as well, and breaks thermodynamics. Ours cannot make current from nothing.

24
00:02:16.181 --> 00:02:22.258
On the KcsA potassium channel, with geometry from its crystal structure,

25
00:02:22.258 --> 00:02:26.844
the model gives a reversal potential of −91.3 mV.

26
00:02:26.894 --> 00:02:30.076
That's the Nernst value, to numerical precision,

27
00:02:30.076 --> 00:02:35.364
across a hundred-fold range of potassium: the check a broken model fails.

28
00:02:36.214 --> 00:02:38.517
Every number records where it came from,

29
00:02:38.517 --> 00:02:43.226
and a mutation audit breaks the code on purpose to prove the tests notice.

30
00:02:43.276 --> 00:02:45.354
We report what didn't work, too.

31
00:02:45.404 --> 00:02:49.033
The KcsA landscape is constrained to published values,

32
00:02:49.033 --> 00:02:53.649
not yet learned, and trapped ions can't pin down the barriers.

33
00:02:53.699 --> 00:02:56.915
Conductance comes out more than 20× too low,

34
00:02:56.915 --> 00:03:03.287
because potassium crosses KcsA by multi-ion knock-on and our model is single-ion.

35
00:03:03.337 --> 00:03:10.675
So we've moved to gramicidin A, where one ion is the right model, now built in a membrane and equilibrated.

36
00:03:11.525 --> 00:03:18.631
So how far can a chain of surrogates take us? Further than we expected, if every link carries its own evidence.

37
00:03:18.681 --> 00:03:22.828
Lightning makes billion-molecule screening an afternoon's work.

38
00:03:22.828 --> 00:03:28.214
The bond-graph pipeline turns atoms into a component physiologists can use.

39
00:03:28.264 --> 00:03:35.276
Next, we join them: screen the library, pose the hits, simulate them, and ask what each one does to a cell.

40
00:03:35.326 --> 00:03:38.285
It's all open source. Thanks for watching.
